  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Elevated tide cycles persist resulting in\nwidespread minor coastal flooding continuing for back bay areas\nin Ocean County, NJ and Sussex County, DE.\n\nThe remnants of Josephine have pulled away from the region as \nit's now over the Canadian Maritimes. However, elevated water \nlevels persist, mainly in the back bay areas of Ocean County, NJ\nand Sussex County, Delaware. The Coastal Flood Advisory for \nthese counties remains in effect until 6 PM today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A couple more rounds of frost impacts are expected\nboth tonight and Sunday night where the growing season continues.\n\nLittle change in the overall synoptic pattern is expected\nthrough tonight with strong surface high pressure essentially\noverhead. In fact, 500 mb temperatures are a touch cooler \ntonight but surface dew points are expected to be a couple of\ndegrees higher tonight. Overall, this should result in low \ntemperatures pretty similar to those observed earlier this \nmorning with lows mainly in the 30s across the region, outside \nof coastal areas and southern DE. For this reason, have issued \nFrost Advisories for the entire region where the growing season \nremains active except for the coastal strip of NJ, Sussex \nCounty DE, and Talbot County, MD. Would not be surprised to see \nsome areas dip into the upper 20s where the growing season \nremains active, more notably across the Pine Barrens of South \nJersey, however, do not believe that enough coverage of sub- \nfreezing temperatures will be met to prompt Freeze Warnings \ntonight in these areas.\n\nTemperatures will then begin to moderate on Sunday before\nanother round of frost is possible for Sunday night into Monday\nmorning. Lows around the region will mainly be in the mid 30s to\nlow 40s. Depending on how tonight's frost advisories pan out\nwill determine where frost advisories may be warranted on Sunday\nnight...more details to come in future updates.",
